For most of history, it seemed obvious that the Sun moved around Earth. But in 499 CE, the Indian mathematician [[@aryabhata|Aryabhata]] wrote that Earth spins on its axis, and that the stars only appear to move. He compared it to a person on a moving boat who sees the trees on the bank moving backwards.

In Europe, the idea that Earth spins and goes around the Sun became widely accepted in the 1600s. In 1851, the French scientist Léon Foucault hung a heavy ball on a long wire in Paris. As the hours passed, the direction of its swing slowly turned, showing that Earth was turning beneath it.

{{story:aryabhata}}

For a long time scientists thought all life depended on sunlight. Then, in 1977, explorers in a small submarine found animals crowded around [[@geysers-hot-springs|hot springs]] on the floor of the Pacific Ocean, far below where any light reaches.

{{story:ocean-deepest}}

Those creatures live on energy from chemicals in the water. If [[@deep-sea-vents|vents like these]] exist on Europa's sea floor, its ocean could have food and warmth without a single ray of sunshine. That doesn't mean anything lives there. It means the idea is worth testing.

The 27 music pieces include Bach and Beethoven, a Peruvian wedding song, Navajo night chants, a song from Azerbaijan and Chuck Berry's rock and roll hit "Johnny B. Goode". One track comes from India: "Jaat Kahan Ho", sung by the Hindustani classical singer Kesarbai Kerkar in a [[@raga-time|raga]] called Bhairavi.

So a three-and-a-half-minute recording of an Indian singer is now among the farthest human-made sounds from Earth. Kesarbai, as it happens, was famous for rarely letting her concerts be recorded at all.

{{story:voyager}}

A goose's blood contains [[haemoglobin|haemoglobin]], the [[protein|protein]] that carries oxygen, just as yours does. But a tiny change in the goose's version makes it hold on to oxygen more tightly, even when there is little of it around. Their lungs are large and, like all birds' lungs, air flows through them in one direction, so fresh air is always passing over. Compare that with the in-and-out breathing inside [[@lungs-breathe|your own lungs]].

Their flight muscles are also packed with tiny blood vessels. That brings oxygen right to the cells doing the hard work of flapping. Their wings are also slightly larger for their weight than those of most geese, which helps them stay up in thin air.

{{story:himalaya-form}}

Seahorses are fish, but they break most fish rules. They swim upright. Their heads point forward at an angle, like a horse's. They have no scales, but a coat of bony plates under their skin. Their curly tails can grip seagrass or coral, which very few other fish tails can do.

Their eyes can move separately, so one can watch for food while the other looks out for danger. They feed by sucking tiny shrimps into their snouts with a quick snap, like a [[@vacuum-cleaners|vacuum cleaner]]. With a small back fin as their main engine, they are among the slowest fish of all. The dwarf seahorse may take an hour to travel a metre and a half.

{{story:penguins-cold}}

For a long time, people had no idea what happened inside a chrysalis. Now scientists can scan them and watch. Most of the caterpillar's [[@muscles|muscles]] and gut [[dissolve|dissolve]]. But small clusters of cells, called imaginal discs, survive. Each one grows into a particular part: one pair becomes the wings, others the legs, antennae or eyes.

Some parts, like the breathing tubes and parts of the [[@nervous-system|nervous system]], are kept and rebuilt rather than destroyed. And some memories may survive too. In 2008, scientists in the USA trained moth caterpillars to avoid a certain smell. When the moths came out of their cases, many still avoided it.

{{story:monarch-migration}}

Mirza Ghalib, who lived in Delhi from 1797 to 1869, is the most quoted Urdu poet. He saw the last days of the [[@mughals|Mughal]] court and the violence of 1857. His ghazals are clever, sad and often funny at his own expense.

One of his most famous couplets begins *"Hazaron khwahishen aisi ke har khwahish pe dam nikle."* Roughly: "Thousands of wishes, each one worth dying for. Many came true, and yet too few." Ghalib's house in Old Delhi's Ballimaran lane is now a small museum.

{{story:urdu}}

In 1782, the Swiss mathematician Leonhard Euler made a guess about arranging items in square grids so that no row or column repeats, a puzzle related to [[@sudoku|Sudoku]]. He believed certain sizes of grid were impossible. For 177 years, nobody proved him wrong.

Then, in 1959, R. C. Bose and S. S. Shrikhande, working in the USA, together with the American mathematician E. T. Parker, showed that Euler was wrong for every size he had doubted except the two smallest. The discovery made the front page of *The New York Times*, and the three became known as "Euler's spoilers". Their work later helped in designing experiments and computer codes.

{{story:kolkata}}

The Indian system groups numbers after the first thousand in steps of a hundred. A lakh has 5 zeros, a crore 7, an arab 9 and a kharab 11. Older lists go further, with words like *neel*, *padma* and *shankh*, though books don't all agree on exactly how big each one is.

Ancient Indian writers loved huge numbers. One [[@buddhist-texts|Buddhist text]], the Lalitavistara, tells of the young Buddha naming numbers in a contest, climbing all the way to one with 53 zeros.

{{story:space-distances}}

Indian mathematicians put three ideas together: ten digits, place value and a symbol for zero to mark an empty place. Without zero, 25, 205 and 250 would look alike. By the time of [[@aryabhata|Aryabhata]], around 499 CE, Indian [[astronomer|astronomers]] were working with place value for huge calculations.

The idea spread west. In the 800s, the Persian scholar al-Khwarizmi wrote a book about calculating with [[@indian-numerals|Indian numerals]]. In 1202, the Italian mathematician Fibonacci, who had learned the system from traders in North Africa, explained it to Europeans in his book *Liber Abaci*. Merchants found it far faster than Roman numerals, though some cities at first banned it in official accounts, worried that the new digits were easy to forge.

{{story:babylon}}

The name comes from Spam, a brand of [[@canned-food|tinned meat]] first sold in the USA in 1937. In a 1970 sketch by the British comedy group Monty Python, a café serves Spam with nearly every dish. A group of Vikings then drown out the conversation by singing "Spam, Spam, Spam".

In the 1980s and 1990s, people on early online chat groups began calling floods of repeated junk messages "spam", because they drowned out everything else, just like the singing Vikings.

{{story:canned-food}}

Before UPI, sending money meant typing long account numbers and bank codes, and waiting. UPI gives every account a simple address, something like name@bankname. You can also pay to a phone number or by scanning a [[@qr-codes|QR code]], which carries that address hidden in its pattern of squares.

Because every bank speaks the same language, it doesn't matter which app you use or which bank the shopkeeper uses. Payments of a few rupees are just as easy as big ones, and ordinary users pay no fee.

{{story:money-banks}}

The Greek historian Arrian tells a famous story. When Porus was brought to Alexander, Alexander asked how he wished to be treated. Porus is said to have replied, "Like a king."

We can't be sure he said exactly this, since Arrian wrote more than 400 years later. He relied on earlier accounts by Alexander's officers, which are now lost. But Alexander did let Porus keep ruling and gave him more land. Alexander needed friends to guard the region after he moved on. Near the battlefield he founded two towns, one named Bucephala after his beloved horse, Bucephalus, who died around this time.

{{story:elephants-talk}}

The steppe was home to many tribes who raided and fought each other. Temujin defeated rivals one by one, including old friends who turned against him. Instead of rewarding only his relatives, he promoted people who were loyal and skilled, even former enemies.

In 1206 a great gathering of Mongol leaders, a *kurultai*, proclaimed him Genghis Khan. He organised his people into units of ten, a hundred and a thousand, broke up old tribal loyalties, and issued rules known as the Yassa. No complete copy of the Yassa survives, so historians know it only from later accounts.

{{story:dna}}

In 1334, he arrived in Delhi, then ruled by Sultan Muhammad bin Tughlaq of the [[@delhi-sultanate|Delhi Sultanate]]. The sultan liked to reward foreign scholars, and appointed Ibn Battuta a judge with a large salary.

Ibn Battuta described the sultan as both generous and frighteningly cruel. He fell from favour once, and feared for his life. Then, in 1341, the sultan sent him to China as an ambassador, with gifts and a large group of attendants. He also wrote about Indian things that amazed him, such as coconuts, [[@food-paan|betel leaves]] and the speed of the royal postal runners.

{{story:maps-history}}

In a Nigerian tale, the birds were invited to a feast in the sky. Tortoise couldn't fly, so he persuaded each bird to lend him a feather. On the way, he told them that everyone at a feast takes a new name, and he chose "All of you".

When the hosts served the food, saying "This is for all of you", Tortoise ate the best of it. The angry birds took back their feathers. Tortoise had to jump down, and his shell cracked into the patchwork pattern it has today. The Nigerian writer Chinua Achebe included this story in his famous novel *Things Fall Apart*.

{{story:mali-empire}}
